                Iterator<?> iterator = iterators[i];
                boolean isNull = iterator == null || !iterator.hasNext();
                nullsOnly &= isNull;
                if (isNull) {
                    rowValues.put(keys[i], null);
                } else {
                    rowValues.put(keys[i], iterator.next());
                }
            }
            if (!nullsOnly) {
                StringBuilder output = new StringBuilder();

                // Build CSV string for the current row
                for (Object key : keys) {
                    if (output.length() > 0) {
                        output.append(',');
                    }
                    Object value = rowValues.get(key);
                    output.append(value != null ? value.toString() : "");
                }

                if (outputList != null) {
                    outputList.add(output.toString());
                } else {
                    if (useRowProcessor) {
                        processRecord(headerMapping, rowValues);
                    } else {
                        writeRow(headerMapping, rowValues);
                    }
                }
                recordCount++;  // Ensure record count is incremented
            }
        } while (!nullsOnly);
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        throw throwExceptionAndClose("Error processing input rows from map", t);
    }
}
```

**Key Points**:
- The output for each row is constructed as a CSV line using `StringBuilder`, directly appending each value.
- Check for `null` values and handle them by appending an empty string in their place.
- Ensure rows are added to `outputList` in the CSV format.

This implementation directly generates CSV-formatted lines without relying on `processRecordToString` and `writeRowToString`, thereby eliminating incorrect output issues related to those methods.
